@Francis
To find out exactly how old it is, I did a google search and, as instructed, looked for a label number on the inside wall of the refrigerator: NH10-JS.  Then I did a search on the G.E. site.  It was made in 1952.  We bought it at an auction around 1972 for $65, and it looked brand new.  The vegetable drawers are porcelain enamel, as are all the other surfaces except the freezer interior, which is aluminum.  It is not "self defrosting," which means it doesn't run all the time and is cheaper to operate.  At some point we replaced the door gaskets ourselves. It has some dings on it now, but that's fine with us.

As the repair man said, "No, they don't make them like that anymore."

@dutchiris

That's impressive.  Your refrigerator and I were born the same year.

I've got a few dings on me, too.

@dutchiris

 I have a blender that I bought 33 years ago in an antique store for $10 that still works. It was manufactured sometime between 1958 and 1965. I use it daily, and have done so for years.

It has two small holes that allow you to put a few drops of oil into the bearings occasionally. I doubt that's commonly done nowadays.

@dutchiris
Our fridge is only 25 years old, but I hope and pray it outlives me.  Nothing fancy -- no ice maker, no water spout, freezer on the bottom, no double doors to open.  It just goes on and on.  Never had any repairs done.

I hear horror stories from family and friends with 5 year old fridges that need all kinds of work, and bless my basic Amana.

